# ============================================================================
# NEW TABLE KIND 1 - THE CROSSING TABLE (order book as two table values)
# ============================================================================
# Every table kind in the tutorials answers "is this row present?". A book
# has to answer a RELATIONAL question instead: "do these two tables admit a
# pair whose prices cross?" The construction keeps both sides as ordinary
# table values over disjoint column streams and puts the matching law in a
# THIRD value, a pure inequality with no rows at all:
#
#   BIDS  = { obpx=5, obq=10 } | { obpx=7, obq=4 }
#   ASKS  = { oapx=6, oaq=8  } | { oapx=9, oaq=3 }
#   LAW   = { obpx >= oapx }                    <- the matching law
#   CROSS = BIDS & ASKS & LAW                   <- the matchable pair set
#
# Because bids and asks use different column streams their meet is the full
# cartesian product, and the law selects the crossing pairs out of it. The
# match is therefore not computed and compared - it is the value CROSS, and
# "the book crosses" is the single decidable question CROSS != 0.
#
# ZenoDEX surface: the CLOB admission gate. An orderbook batch can be
# admitted only if CROSS is non-empty, and the specific pair that clears is
# the membership witness (b2) - both decided without a matching engine.
# Run: tau -q < opus_new1_crossing_table.tau
# EXPECTED-RESULTS: T T T T T F

set charvar off
set maxsplits 1

# (1) both sides are live tables:
n ({ obpx[t]:bv[8] = { #x05 }:bv[8] && obq[t]:bv[8] = { #x0A }:bv[8] } | { obpx[t]:bv[8] = { #x07 }:bv[8] && obq[t]:bv[8] = { #x04 }:bv[8] }) != 0
# Answer: T

n ({ oapx[t]:bv[8] = { #x06 }:bv[8] && oaq[t]:bv[8] = { #x08 }:bv[8] } | { oapx[t]:bv[8] = { #x09 }:bv[8] && oaq[t]:bv[8] = { #x03 }:bv[8] }) != 0
# Answer: T

# (2) THE BOOK CROSSES: CROSS = BIDS & ASKS & LAW is non-empty.
n (({ obpx[t]:bv[8] = { #x05 }:bv[8] && obq[t]:bv[8] = { #x0A }:bv[8] } | { obpx[t]:bv[8] = { #x07 }:bv[8] && obq[t]:bv[8] = { #x04 }:bv[8] }) & ({ oapx[t]:bv[8] = { #x06 }:bv[8] && oaq[t]:bv[8] = { #x08 }:bv[8] } | { oapx[t]:bv[8] = { #x09 }:bv[8] && oaq[t]:bv[8] = { #x03 }:bv[8] }) & { obpx[t]:bv[8] >= oapx[t]:bv[8] }) != 0
# Answer: T - bid 7 meets ask 6.

# (3) THE CLEARING PAIR IS FORCED: CROSS entails (bid 7, ask 6). This is the
# membership test, not overlap - the meet with the complement is empty, so
# no other pair is in CROSS. The book has ONE crossing pair, provably.
n ((({ obpx[t]:bv[8] = { #x05 }:bv[8] && obq[t]:bv[8] = { #x0A }:bv[8] } | { obpx[t]:bv[8] = { #x07 }:bv[8] && obq[t]:bv[8] = { #x04 }:bv[8] }) & ({ oapx[t]:bv[8] = { #x06 }:bv[8] && oaq[t]:bv[8] = { #x08 }:bv[8] } | { oapx[t]:bv[8] = { #x09 }:bv[8] && oaq[t]:bv[8] = { #x03 }:bv[8] }) & { obpx[t]:bv[8] >= oapx[t]:bv[8] }) & { obpx[t]:bv[8] = { #x07 }:bv[8] && oapx[t]:bv[8] = { #x06 }:bv[8] }') = 0
# Answer: T

# (4) UNSAT CONTROL - a book whose only bid is 5 does NOT cross asks at 6/9:
n (({ obpx[t]:bv[8] = { #x05 }:bv[8] && obq[t]:bv[8] = { #x0A }:bv[8] }) & ({ oapx[t]:bv[8] = { #x06 }:bv[8] && oaq[t]:bv[8] = { #x08 }:bv[8] } | { oapx[t]:bv[8] = { #x09 }:bv[8] && oaq[t]:bv[8] = { #x03 }:bv[8] }) & { obpx[t]:bv[8] >= oapx[t]:bv[8] }) = 0
# Answer: T - no crossing pair exists; the batch must not clear.

# (5) UNSAT CONTROL 2 - the law is what does the work. Drop LAW and the same
# non-crossing book "matches" (the bare product is non-empty), so a T here
# would be meaningless without (4):
n (({ obpx[t]:bv[8] = { #x05 }:bv[8] && obq[t]:bv[8] = { #x0A }:bv[8] }) & ({ oapx[t]:bv[8] = { #x06 }:bv[8] && oaq[t]:bv[8] = { #x08 }:bv[8] } | { oapx[t]:bv[8] = { #x09 }:bv[8] && oaq[t]:bv[8] = { #x03 }:bv[8] })) = 0
# Answer: F - without the matching law every bid "matches" every ask.
